---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { release, download, releaseURL } from '../data/site';
const linux = release.binaries.find(b => b.file.includes('linux-amd64'))!;
const windows = release.binaries.find(b => b.platform === 'windows')!;
---
<Guide title="Install Livewire" description="One CLI, running on your machine. Start with a verified release for Windows or Linux." sections={[{id:'download',label:'Download'},{id:'verify',label:'Verify the artifact'},{id:'linux',label:'Linux'},{id:'windows',label:'Windows'},{id:'first-run',label:'First run'}]}>
  <h2 id="download">Get v{release.version}</h2><p>Choose the binary for your system. Linux ARM64 artifacts are cross-built; the software-lab runtime qualification covers Windows and Linux x64.</p><div class="download-list">{release.binaries.map(binary => <a class="download-item" href={download(binary.file)}><div><strong>{binary.label}</strong><small>{binary.file}</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>)}</div><p><a href={releaseURL}>All release assets ↗</a> · <a href={download('SHA256SUMS')}>SHA256SUMS</a></p>
  <h2 id="verify">Verify before you run</h2><p>Download the checksum manifest alongside the artifact. Compare the exact filename and SHA-256 digest. A checksum detects changed bytes; a provenance attestation also identifies the GitHub build workflow.</p><Code label="Linux · verify the downloaded binary" code={`curl --fail --location --output ${linux.file} \\\n  ${download(linux.file)}\ncurl --fail --location --output SHA256SUMS \\\n  ${download('SHA256SUMS')}\ngrep '  ${linux.file}$' SHA256SUMS | sha256sum --check -`} /><Code label="GitHub CLI · verify build provenance" code={`gh attestation verify ${linux.file} --repo kvmukilan/livewire`} /><p>Stop if verification fails. Use the <a href="/releases/">release record</a> to check the version, artifact name, and qualification scope.</p>
  <h2 id="linux">Linux</h2><Code code={`chmod +x ${linux.file}\n./${linux.file} version`} label="Make the verified binary executable" /><p>Rename or install the binary as <code>livewire</code> somewhere on your PATH. Fresh socket-based application replay needs no packet driver or elevated permissions.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Packet injection needs network privileges.</strong>For stateless replay and raw packet routes, use <code>sudo</code> or the documented Linux capabilities on the selected interface. Use a lab network where the captured destinations are appropriate.</div>
  <h2 id="windows">Windows</h2><p>Download the Windows ZIP and <code>SHA256SUMS</code>. This PowerShell example stops if the ZIP does not match its checksum, before extracting or running it.</p><Code label="PowerShell · verify and extract" code={`$entry = @(Get-Content .\\SHA256SUMS | Where-Object {\n  $_.EndsWith('  ${windows.file}')\n})\nif ($entry.Count -ne 1) { throw 'Manifest entry missing or ambiguous' }\n$expected = $entry[0].Split(' ')[0]\n$actual = (Get-FileHash .\\${windows.file} -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()\nif ($actual -ne $expected) { throw 'Checksum mismatch' }\nExpand-Archive .\\${windows.file} -DestinationPath .\\livewire\n.\\livewire\\livewire.exe version`} /><p>Fresh application sessions work without Npcap or Administrator rights. For packet injection, install <a href="https://npcap.com/">Npcap</a> and follow the included <code>setup-windows.ps1</code> instructions as Administrator.</p>
  <h2 id="first-run">Inspect your first capture</h2><p>Inspection is offline. It does not send the capture to a target or upload it to this website.</p><Code code={"livewire check issue.pcap -details\nlivewire help live"} label="Inspect and discover" /><p>Then choose <a href="/workflows/">fresh application replay or stateless packet replay</a>. For a browser interface on your own machine, run <code>livewire web</code>.</p><p class="reference-link">More setup details: <a href="/reference/setup/">version-pinned installation guide →</a></p>
</Guide>
